Chapter: BoundedAccepted
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model 3.3, the arm that must COMPILE. `bounded-exceeded` in
 `codex/test/errors` proves the refusal; a refusal arm on its own cannot tell
 a working check from one that refuses everything, so this is the control.

 `push-loop` accumulates with `list-push`, which extends in place under
 capacity and doubles when it is not, so the accumulator is not copied per
 iteration and the definition is not `growing`. `walks-ok` declares
 `bounded linear` over it and holds. The two chapters differ in ONE thing:
 the callee's accumulator operator.

Section: A Callee That Holds

  push-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  push-loop (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else push-loop n (i + 1) (list-push acc i)

Section: The Declaration That Holds

  bounded linear walks-ok : Integer -> Integer
  walks-ok (n) = list-length (push-loop n 0 [])

Section: A Ceiling Nothing Can Breach

 `growing` is the top of the lattice, so it accepts anything, including the
 shape the other chapter is refused for. It is here so the arm covers a
 satisfied ceiling as well as a satisfying body.

  bounded growing grows-declared : Integer -> Text
  grows-declared (n) = grow-loop n 0 ""

  grow-loop : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  grow-loop (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else grow-loop n (i + 1) (acc & "x")

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("walks-ok 8 = " & show (walks-ok 8))
    print-line-uni ("grows-declared 4 = " & grows-declared 4)
  end
